---
import Highlight from '../../layouts/Highlight.astro';
import Meta from '../../components/Meta.astro';
---

<Highlight slug="taming-memory" lede="How I tracked down why a large Next.js app kept running out of memory — and why raising the limit was never the fix.">
	<Meta
		items={[
			['Where', 'adventure.inc, Adventure Dhaka Limited'],
			['Type', 'Production audit'],
			['Stack', 'Next.js, Node.js, AWS'],
			['Read', '~4 min'],
		]}
	/>
	<section style="padding-top: 64px">
		<h2 class="lbl">The problem</h2>
		<div class="prose">
			<p>Under load, the app's server pods kept crashing with <strong>“JavaScript heap out of memory”</strong>. The tempting fix is to give Node more memory. That only moves the crash later, and makes every crash more expensive.</p>
			<p>So the question wasn't <em>how much memory do we need</em>. It was <strong>what is holding on to memory that it should have let go?</strong></p>
		</div>
	</section>
	<section style="padding-top: 64px">
		<h2 class="lbl">How I looked for it</h2>
		<div class="prose">
			<p>I listed everything that could keep memory alive across a request, then ranked each suspect by two things: how likely it was to cause these crashes, and how much memory it could hold at its worst. That gave three tiers, and I worked strictly from the top.</p>
		</div>
	</section>
	<section style="padding-top: 64px">
		<h2 class="lbl">What I found</h2>
		<div class="tier">
			<div class="tierh"><span class="mono tiern">Tier 1</span><span class="tiert">Requests that never let go</span></div>
			<ul class="bl">
				<li><span><b>Timeouts and retries that multiplied.</b> Outgoing calls waited up to 120 seconds, and a retry setting overrode the safe default. In the worst case, one slow upstream call could keep its data in memory for about <b>494 seconds</b>.</span></li>
				<li><span><b>No circuit breaker.</b> When a dependency slowed down, new requests kept piling onto it, each one holding memory while it waited.</span></li>
			</ul>
		</div>
		<div class="tier">
			<div class="tierh"><span class="mono tiern">Tier 2</span><span class="tiert">Whole files in memory</span></div>
			<ul class="bl">
				<li><span><b>Image processing</b> used roughly <b>4×</b> the file size in memory, with heavier formats like HEIC on top.</span></li>
				<li><span><b>File proxying</b> loaded entire files into memory instead of streaming them through.</span></li>
			</ul>
		</div>
		<div class="tier">
			<div class="tierh"><span class="mono tiern">Tier 3</span><span class="tiert">Work the server didn't need to do</span></div>
			<ul class="bl">
				<li><span><b>Deep copies</b> of large responses in <b>27 places</b>, each one doubling that data for a moment.</span></li>
				<li><span><b>22 pages forced to render on every request</b>, skipping caching that would have taken load off entirely.</span></li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</section>
	<section style="padding-top: 64px">
		<h2 class="lbl">Seeing it coming</h2>
		<div class="prose">
			<p>Fixes help; visibility keeps them working. I added a health check that compares the real heap against the container's memory limit. When it gets close, the pod reports itself unhealthy and stops taking traffic — <strong>before</strong> it crashes, not after.</p>
		</div>
	</section>
	<section style="padding-top: 64px">
		<h2 class="lbl">The fix plan</h2>
		<ul class="bl">
			<li><span>Cap timeouts and retries, and add a circuit breaker for slow dependencies.</span></li>
			<li><span>Stream files instead of buffering them, and limit upload sizes.</span></li>
			<li><span>Remove unnecessary deep copies.</span></li>
			<li><span>Cache pages that never needed to be dynamic.</span></li>
		</ul>
		<div class="note" style="margin-top: 16px">[CONFIRM WHICH OF THESE SHIPPED, AND ADD ANYTHING ELSE YOU CHANGED]</div>
	</section>
	<section style="padding-top: 64px">
		<h2 class="lbl">Results</h2>
		<div class="stat">
			<div><span class="n">[X]%</span><span class="l">[METRIC — e.g. lower peak heap]</span></div>
			<div><span class="n">[X]</span><span class="l">[METRIC — e.g. fewer restarts per week]</span></div>
			<div><span class="n">0</span><span class="l">[CONFIRM — OOM crashes since]</span></div>
		</div>
	</section>
	<section style="padding-top: 64px">
		<h2 class="lbl">What I took from it</h2>
		<div class="prose">
			<p><strong>Don't raise the limit; find what's holding on.</strong> Memory problems are rarely one big leak. They are a few reasonable decisions that become dangerous together, under load, on a bad day.</p>
		</div>
	</section>
</Highlight>
